$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: template-website/winning-trimming-com-au/hostinger-deploy
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-15"
summary: >-
  Production deployment to Hostinger VPS (VM 1607392, IP 187.77.152.132) via Docker Compose +
  Traefik. GitHub Actions CI/CD (deploy.yml) syncs code via rsync, builds Next.js on the VPS in a
  sidecar container with DB access for SSG, builds the Docker image, and restarts the stack with
  --env-file .env.production. Media files persist in a Docker volume mounted at /app/public/media
  (Payload staticDir), surviving container rebuilds. The VPS is SHARED with smith-gray-ai and other
  projects — builds must not exhaust resources. Database migrations must be run before the Next.js
  build or the build fails on SSG pages that query missing tables.
$system: template-website/system
$service: template-website/winning-trimming-com-au
intent: >-
  Go live with the production site so customers can browse the SEO matrix, submit quotes, and staff
  can manage bookings via the Payload admin panel. Automated via GitHub Actions on push to main.
status: built
flows:
  - description: Prepare the VPS, create production compose config, and push the code.
    id: prep-and-deploy
    name: Pre-deploy preparation
    steps:
      - action: snapshot
        id: s1
        target: Take a VPS snapshot before deploying
      - action: create
        id: s2
        target: Create docker-compose.prod.yml with Traefik labels, production env, and built image
      - action: create
        id: s3
        target: Create .env.production with all production credentials
      - action: build
        id: s4
        target: Build the production Docker image on the VPS
      - action: start
        id: s5
        target: Start containers with docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d
  - description: Update GoDaddy DNS to point winningtrimming.com.au to the VPS.
    id: dns-update
    name: Update GoDaddy DNS
    steps:
      - action: get
        id: s1
        target: Fetch current DNS records for winningtrimming.com.au via GoDaddy API
      - action: update
        id: s2
        target: Update A record @ to 187.77.152.132
      - action: update
        id: s3
        target: Update A record www to 187.77.152.132
      - action: preserve
        id: s4
        target: Preserve MX records (Google Workspace) and TXT records (SPF, domain verification)
      - action: wait
        id: s5
        target: Wait for DNS propagation (TTL 600s, should be under 10 minutes)
  - description: Run database migration and seed the database.
    id: migrate-and-seed
    name: Database migration and seed
    steps:
      - action: run
        id: s1
        target: Run pnpm migrate inside the web container to apply all migrations (BEFORE the build)
      - action: navigate
        id: s2
        target: Visit https://winningtrimming.com.au/admin and create the first admin user
      - action: run
        id: s3
        target: Run the seed endpoint to populate pages, posts, projects, matrix, regions, suburbs
      - action: run
        id: s4
        target: Run the empty-pillars seed script to create caravan/trade/commercial asset types
      - action: run
        id: s5
        target: Run the Google reviews sync script to fetch reviews from Places API
  - description: Configure the daily cron job for Google reviews sync.
    id: cron-setup
    name: Configure reviews cron
    steps:
      - action: create
        id: s1
        target: Create cron entry on VPS for daily reviews sync at 6am
      - action: create
        id: s2
        target: Ensure environment variables are available to cron
  - description: Verify the site is live and all pages return 200.
    id: verify
    name: Post-deploy verification
    steps:
      - action: check
        id: s1
        target: curl https://winningtrimming.com.au returns 200
      - action: check
        id: s2
        target: curl https://winningtrimming.com.au/quote returns 200
      - action: check
        id: s3
        target: curl https://winningtrimming.com.au/admin returns 200
      - action: check
        id: s4
        target: curl https://winningtrimming.com.au/marine returns 200
      - action: check
        id: s5
        target: curl https://winningtrimming.com.au/sitemap.xml returns 200
      - action: check
        id: s6
        target: Verify SSL certificate is valid
  - description: >-
      Safe deploy procedure for CI/CD and manual deploys. The VPS is shared with smith-gray-ai and
      other projects — builds must not crash the server.
    id: safe-deploy-procedure
    name: Safe deploy procedure (CI/CD and manual)
    steps:
      - action: check
        id: s1
        target: >-
          SSH into VPS and check system load: `uptime`. If 1-min load average > 4.0, ABORT and retry
          later. The VPS has 4 vCPU — load > 4 means CPU-bound.
      - action: check
        id: s2
        target: >-
          Check available memory: `free -h`. If available < 2GB, ABORT and retry later. The build
          sidecar needs ~2GB for pnpm + Next.js.
      - action: check
        id: s3
        target: >-
          Check no other build is running: `docker ps --filter ancestor=node:20-slim -q`. If output
          is non-empty, ABORT — another deploy is in progress.
      - action: snapshot
        id: s4
        target: >-
          Take a VPS snapshot via hapi: `hapi vps snapshots create 1607392`. This is the rollback
          point.
      - action: rsync
        id: s5
        target: >-
          Sync code to VPS via rsync (excludes node_modules, .next, .git, storage, public/media,
          .env*)
      - action: run
        id: s6
        target: >-
          Run database migrations INSIDE the existing web container: `docker exec
          winningtrimming-web-1 pnpm migrate`. This ensures tables exist BEFORE the build tries to
          query them.
      - action: build
        id: s7
        target: >-
          Start the sidecar build container with --memory=4g limit: `docker run --rm --memory=4g
          --network <net> -v <project>:/build ...`. The memory limit prevents the build from
          consuming all VPS RAM.
      - action: verify
        id: s8
        target: >-
          Verify .next/BUILD_ID exists after build. If not, the build failed — ABORT, do not restart
          containers.
      - action: build
        id: s9
        target: >-
          Build the Docker image: `docker compose --env-file .env.production -f
          docker-compose.prod.yml build web`
      - action: restart
        id: s10
        target: >-
          Restart the stack: `docker compose --env-file .env.production -f docker-compose.prod.yml
          up -d`
      - action: cleanup
        id: s11
        target: >-
          Prune build artifacts: `docker container prune -f && docker image prune -f`. This frees
          disk space and prevents buildup.
      - action: health
        id: s12
        target: >-
          Health check: poll http://localhost:3000/ inside the web container until 200 or timeout
          (90s)
      - action: cleanup
        id: s13
        target: >-
          On ANY failure or cancellation: kill lingering build containers: `docker ps --filter
          ancestor=node:20-slim --filter status=running -q | xargs -r docker kill`. This prevents
          orphaned builds from consuming resources.
contracts:
  - description: Production docker-compose.yml on the VPS with Postgres, Next.js app, and Traefik routing
    id: docker-compose-prod
    must_have:
      - Postgres 16 service with persistent volume
      - Web service built from Dockerfile.prod
      - Traefik labels for winningtrimming.com.au with SSL
      - POSTGRES_URI uses Docker service name db
      - Compose launched with --env-file .env.production so interpolation resolves correctly
      - Storage volume mounted at /app/public/media (Payload staticDir) not /app/storage
      - Media files persist across container rebuilds via Docker volume
    applies_after:
      - compose file written
  - description: Media files (seed images + uploads) persist across container rebuilds
    id: media-persistence
    must_have:
      - Docker volume winningtrimming_storage mounted at /app/public/media
      - Volume pre-populated with all seed media from local public/media tar
      - Payload Media collection staticDir = public/media matches the volume mount
      - New uploads survive docker compose down/up
    applies_after:
      - first deploy
  - description: Next.js build runs on the VPS in a sidecar container with DB access for SSG
    id: build-on-vps
    must_have:
      - Build container joins the compose network to reach db service
      - DB migration prompt (batch=-1) must not block the build
      - .next/BUILD_ID exists after build before image is built
      - Dockerfile.prod COPYs the pre-built .next into the image
    applies_after:
      - build script written
  - description: GitHub Actions CI/CD for automated deploys
    id: github-actions-cicd
    must_have:
      - "deploy.yml: SSH into VPS, rsync code, build, restart, health check"
      - "ci.yml: lint + typecheck on PRs"
      - "Secrets: VPS_HOST, VPS_USER, VPS_SSH_KEY, VPS_PROJECT_DIR"
      - "--env-file .env.production used for all compose commands"
      - 30-minute timeout on the deploy job
      - >-
        On failure/cancellation, kill any lingering build containers: docker ps --filter name=build
        --filter status=running -q | xargs -r docker kill
    applies_after:
      - workflow written
  - description: Traefik handles HTTPS with Let's Encrypt for winningtrimming.com.au
    id: ssl-cert
    must_have:
      - Traefik ACME configuration for Let's Encrypt
      - Automatic redirect from HTTP to HTTPS
      - Certificate auto-renewal
    applies_after:
      - Traefik configured
  - description: Site is accessible and all pages return 200
    id: verify-live
    must_have:
      - https://winningtrimming.com.au returns 200
      - https://winningtrimming.com.au/admin returns 200
      - All 276 media files return 200 via /api/media/file/
      - Homepage, quote, admin, pillar pages return 200
    applies_after:
      - deploy complete
  - description: The VPS is a SHARED resource. Deploys must be resource-aware and not crash other projects.
    id: vps-shared-resource-safety
    must_have:
      - >-
        Before starting a build, SSH in and check `uptime` — if load average (1min) > 4.0, abort and
        retry later
      - Before starting a build, check `free -h` — if available memory < 2GB, abort and retry later
      - >-
        The deploy workflow must NOT run in parallel with other project deploys on the same VPS
        (smith-gray-ai deploys to the same VM 1607392)
      - >-
        On failure or cancellation, kill any lingering build sidecar containers: docker ps --filter
        ancestor=node:20-slim --filter status=running -q | xargs -r docker kill
      - >-
        The build sidecar container must have a memory limit (docker run --memory=4g) to prevent it
        from consuming all VPS RAM
      - >-
        After the build completes, prune the build container and any dangling images: docker
        container prune -f && docker image prune -f
    applies_after:
      - deploy workflow run
  - description: >-
      Database migrations must be run BEFORE the Next.js build, not after. The build generates
      static pages that query the database.
    id: migrate-before-build
    must_have:
      - >-
        The deploy workflow runs `pnpm migrate` (or `payload migrate`) inside the web container
        BEFORE starting the sidecar build container
      - If a migration fails, the deploy aborts — do not build on top of a missing table
      - >-
        New Payload auth collections (with auth: {}) require a sessions table migration — check for
        this when adding new auth-enabled collections
      - >-
        The migration step runs inside the existing web container (which has DB access), not in the
        sidecar build container
      - Migration output is logged to the GitHub Actions output for debugging
    applies_after:
      - deploy workflow run
  - description: How to update data schemas (Payload collections) in production without breaking the site
    id: schema-update-procedure
    must_have:
      - "Step 1: Create migration file locally (pnpm migrate:create)"
      - "Step 2: Test migration locally against a fresh DB (pnpm migrate && pnpm seed)"
      - "Step 3: Commit migration file to the repo"
      - "Step 4: Push to main — the deploy workflow will run migrations before the build"
      - "Step 5: Monitor the GitHub Actions run — if migration fails, SSH in and fix manually"
      - NEVER use push:true in production postgresAdapter — always use explicit migrations
      - >-
        NEVER run pnpm migrate directly on the VPS outside the deploy workflow — it may conflict
        with a concurrent deploy
      - >-
        If a migration adds a new table that SSG pages query, the migration MUST be in the same
        deploy as the code that queries it
    applies_after:
      - schema change needed
tests:
  - id: home-page-live
    setup:
      url: https://winningtrimming.com.au
    expect:
      - result: "200"
  - id: admin-accessible
    setup:
      url: https://winningtrimming.com.au/admin
    expect:
      - result: "200"
  - id: quote-form-live
    setup:
      url: https://winningtrimming.com.au/quote
    expect:
      - result: "200"
  - id: matrix-live
    setup:
      url: https://winningtrimming.com.au/marine/yachts
    expect:
      - result: "200"
  - id: ssl-valid
    setup:
      domain: winningtrimming.com.au
    expect:
      - result: valid-cert
  - id: db-migrated
    setup:
      check: migration-status
    expect:
      - count: 9
        result: migrations-applied
  - id: dns-points-to-vps
    setup:
      check: dig A record
    expect:
      - result: 187.77.152.132
  - id: mx-preserved
    setup:
      check: dig MX record
    expect:
      - result: aspmx.l.google.com
  - id: sitemap-live
    setup:
      url: https://winningtrimming.com.au/sitemap.xml
    expect:
      - result: "200"
  - id: llms-txt-live
    setup:
      url: https://winningtrimming.com.au/llms.txt
    expect:
      - result: "200"
decisions:
  - id: use-existing-vps
    decision: Deploy to existing Hostinger VPS (VM 1607392) rather than a new VPS
    rationale: >-
      The existing VPS has Docker and Traefik already configured, 4 CPU and 16GB RAM which is
      sufficient, and is already running at 187.77.152.132. No need to provision new infrastructure.
    date: "2026-09-07"
  - id: traefik-for-ssl
    decision: Use Traefik for reverse proxy and SSL rather than nginx or Caddy
    rationale: >-
      Traefik is already installed on the VPS (Ubuntu 24.04 with Docker and Traefik template). It
      handles automatic Let's Encrypt certificates and routing via Docker labels.
    date: "2026-09-07"
  - id: godaddy-dns-only
    decision: Update GoDaddy DNS A record to point to VPS rather than transferring nameservers
    rationale: >-
      Domain uses GoDaddy nameservers. Only the A record needs to change from Squarespace IPs to the
      VPS IP. MX records for Google Workspace must be preserved.
    date: "2026-09-07"
  - id: prod-compose-separate
    decision: Use a production docker-compose.yml separate from the local dev compose
    rationale: >-
      Production needs Traefik labels, production env, no volume bind mounts, and built image.
      Keeping it separate avoids dev config in production.
    date: "2026-09-07"
  - id: manual-migration
    decision: Run database migration and seed manually on first deploy
    rationale: >-
      Migration and seed are one-time operations. Manual control ensures they run exactly once,
      avoiding data loss.
    date: "2026-09-07"
  - id: customers-sessions-migration
    decision: >-
      Added migration 20260915_070000_customers_sessions to create the customers_sessions table in
      production, mirroring the earlier users_sessions fix (20260908_171500). The customers
      collection has auth: {}, so Payload's Postgres adapter always left-joins customers_sessions
      when reading customer docs — even at depth=0. Without the table, every GET/POST /api/customers
      failed with PostgreSQL 42P01 (relation does not exist), which Payload's REST layer swallowed
      as a generic 500.
    rationale: >-
      The table was created by dev-mode schema-push locally but never captured in a production
      migration. The users_sessions migration fixed the same gap for the users collection but the
      customers collection was missed. Both auth collections now have explicit session-table
      migrations.
    date: "2026-09-15"
  - id: vps-is-shared-resource
    decision: >-
      The VPS (VM 1607392) is a SHARED resource running 28+ containers across 5 projects
      (smith-gray-ai, winningtrimming, conductor-academy, daangerdainger-staging, traefik). Builds
      and deploys must be resource-aware.
    rationale: >-
      On 2026-09-15, a winningtrimming deploy ran a 30-minute pnpm build in a sidecar container
      while smith-gray-ai was also building. The combined load (load average 14.24 on 4 vCPU) made
      the VPS unresponsive — all sites returned Cloudflare 524 (origin timeout). The VPS had to be
      force-restarted via the Hostinger API, taking down all 5 projects simultaneously. Agents
      deploying to this VPS MUST check current load before starting a build and MUST NOT run builds
      in parallel with other projects.
    date: "2026-09-15"
  - id: migrate-before-build
    decision: >-
      Database migrations MUST be run before the Next.js build, not after. The build process
      generates static pages (SSG) that query the database — if tables are missing, the build fails
      or produces broken pages.
    rationale: >-
      On 2026-09-15, a deploy failed because the customers_sessions table didn't exist in
      production. The Next.js build tried to query customers during SSG, hit PostgreSQL 42P01
      (relation does not exist), and the build either failed or generated broken pages. The fix was
      to add the migration and ensure it runs before the build step. The deploy workflow must run
      migrations BEFORE the sidecar build container.
    date: "2026-09-15"
  - id: check-vps-load-before-deploy
    decision: >-
      Before starting any build on the VPS, check the system load average. If load > 4.0 (1 vCPU per
      core), abort the deploy and retry later.
    rationale: >-
      The VPS has 4 vCPU. A load average above 4.0 means the system is CPU-bound. Running a pnpm
      build (which is CPU-intensive: TypeScript compilation, webpack, SSG page generation) on top of
      an already-loaded system will cause cascading failures — Docker containers start timing out,
      Traefik can't route, and all sites go down. The deploy workflow should SSH in, check `uptime`,
      and abort if load is too high.
    date: "2026-09-15"
  - id: deploy-timeout-30min
    decision: >-
      The deploy workflow has a 30-minute timeout. If the build takes longer, the job is cancelled
      but the sidecar build container may still be running on the VPS, consuming resources.
    rationale: >-
      On 2026-09-15, a deploy was cancelled after 30 minutes but the build container kept running,
      consuming CPU and memory. This contributed to the VPS crash. The deploy workflow should kill
      any lingering build containers on failure/cancellation.
    date: "2026-09-15"
